00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:08.900
<v Orlando>Welcome to <lang pt-BR>Tá Falado</lang>, the Brazilian Portuguese
pronunciation for speakers of Spanish.

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:13.222
<v Orlando>This podcast is part of BrazilPod and comes to you from the

00:00:13.222 --> 00:00:18.000
<v Orlando>College of Liberal Arts here at
the University of Texas in Austin.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:24.940
<v Orlando>Well, we'd like to welcome everybody to the
supplementary lesson number one of <lang pt-BR>Tá Falado</lang>.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:31.420
<v Orlando>These are our lessons where we're trying to give Spanish speakers
and English speakers a sense to hear what Portuguese sounds like.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.930
<v Orlando>Today's lesson is a little bit
different as a supplementary lesson.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:39.097
<v Orlando>Rather than have our normal dialogues and
interchange, it might be a little boring,

00:00:39.097 --> 00:00:45.000
<v Orlando>but what we're going to do is just read some words because
we want people to have a sense of what are we talking about.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.351
<v Orlando>We're not trying to make everybody hear linguists,

00:00:47.351 --> 00:00:50.800
<v Orlando>but it will help to hear the words and
to hear the sounds we're talking about.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.480
<v Orlando>And people can go back to this
recording to hear those sounds later on.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.210
<v Orlando>As always, before we get started, let's introduce everyone.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:00:59.790
<v Orlando>My name is Orlando Kelm.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.550
<v Orlando>I'm a faculty member here at the University
of Texas, and I'm accompanied by...

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.000
<v Michelle>Michelle Lima, I'm from Brazil.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.000
<v Valdo>Valdo Oliveira, I'm from Brazil too.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.750
<v José Luis>I'm José Luis Montiel from Maracaibo, Venezuela.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:18.250
<v Orlando>Well, good. What we're going to do then to start off
is I'm going to read some of the English vowel sounds.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:24.750
<v Orlando>And then after that, we'll have Valdo, Michelle do some
Portuguese sounds and José Luis to do some of our Spanish sounds.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:34.500
<v Orlando>When we're talking about English sounds, first off we
have some vowels. For example, "ee" as in heed, heat,

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:36.000
<v Orlando>bead,

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:37.100
<v Orlando>beat.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:45.000
<v Orlando>"Ih" as in hid, hit, bid, bit.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:47.050
<v Orlando>"Ay"

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.000
<v Orlando>as in hayed, hate,

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:51.800
<v Orlando>bayed,

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:56.750
<v Orlando>bait. And you'll notice that that one is almost
always pronounced as a diphthong in English.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:02.000
<v Orlando>We have the sound "eh" as in head,

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:04.750
<v Orlando>bed, bet.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:10.250
<v Orlando>"A" as in had, hat,

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.250
<v Orlando>bad, bat.

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:21.500
<v Orlando>"Ah" as in hod, hot, body, bought.

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:30.131
<v Orlando>Now, not in my dialect, but other dialects, for
example, in the northeast of the United States,

00:02:30.131 --> 00:02:34.500
<v Orlando>people also have the sound "aw" as in hawed, hot, bought.

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:39.750
<v Orlando>We have the sound "oh" as in hoed,

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:41.000
<v Orlando>bode,

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:42.100
<v Orlando>boat.

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:45.250
<v Orlando>And that one is almost always pronounced as a diphthong as well.

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:50.750
<v Orlando>"U" as in hood, put.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:53.800
<v Orlando>"Oo" as in

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:54.500
<v Orlando>who'd,

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:55.750
<v Orlando>hoot,

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:57.000
<v Orlando>boot.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:02.250
<v Orlando>"Uh" as in HUD, hut,

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:06.250
<v Orlando>bud, but, above.

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:12.250
<v Orlando>Also in English we have a lot of vowel
sounds that have what is called r-coloring.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:17.500
<v Orlando>Those would be sounds like "er" as in herd,

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:18.600
<v Orlando>Hurt.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:20.000
<v Orlando>Bird.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:21.350
<v Orlando>Bert.

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:26.250
<v Orlando>There's also the sound "ar" as in hard,

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:27.500
<v Orlando>Heart.

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:29.000
<v Orlando>Barred.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:30.100
<v Orlando>Bart.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.000
<v Orlando>We also have "ear" as in hear,

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:36.500
<v Orlando>Beard.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:37.750
<v Orlando>Beer.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:43.250
<v Orlando>"Air" as in hair, bear,

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:44.500
<v Orlando>bared,

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.250
<v Orlando>and "ire" as in hired.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.000
<v Orlando>Finally, we should give you the sounds
of some of the diphthongs in English.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:56.750
<v Orlando>We have "I" as in hide,

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:00.500
<v Orlando>height, bide, bite.

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:04.000
<v Orlando>"Oy" as in Boyd,

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:13.000
<v Orlando>"Ow" as in how, bowed, and "you" as in hued.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.480
<v Orlando>And so those are the English sounds.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.596
<v Orlando>Now let's move on to some of the Portuguese vowel sounds,

00:04:18.596 --> 00:04:22.250
<v Orlando>and we're going to ask Valdo and Michelle
